WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:05.680
Now moving on to the second scenario.

00:05.680 --> 00:12.400
To the next use case of using views in projects is that we use views in order to hide complexity and

00:12.400 --> 00:14.000
to improve abstraction.

00:14.000 --> 00:20.040
In many scenarios, we work with a very large and complex databases, and we can use views in order

00:20.040 --> 00:24.000
to reduce the complexity and make things easier for the users.

00:24.000 --> 00:25.480
So let's understand what this means.

00:25.520 --> 00:29.400
Now I'm going to explain for you a scenario that happens almost in each project.

00:29.400 --> 00:33.880
Like if you get an access to a database where you want to do analysis, you will be in a scenario.

00:33.880 --> 00:39.040
And this can happen a lot where you're going to find a large database where the tables are very complex

00:39.040 --> 00:39.680
to understand.

00:39.720 --> 00:41.080
They have a lot of columns.

00:41.080 --> 00:47.320
They have like technical and critical names and how the tables are connected to each other and the relationship

00:47.320 --> 00:48.200
between them.

00:48.200 --> 00:50.240
It's almost impossible to understand.

00:50.240 --> 00:56.520
Then you have to be deeply involved with the data models, which documentations and with experts until

00:56.520 --> 00:59.560
you understand how to query this database.

00:59.560 --> 01:04.750
So if you are not a developer and from end user perspective, it's going to be nightmare where you are

01:04.750 --> 01:08.230
trying to do multiple joints in order to make simple analyses.

01:08.230 --> 01:13.870
And of course, from the database perspective, this data model is good enough for one application.

01:13.870 --> 01:19.630
But if you are opening your database for multiple data analysis projects, this can be a nightmare because

01:19.630 --> 01:23.470
you have to go and explain for each user how to query the data.

01:23.550 --> 01:29.990
So what we usually do, instead of giving a direct access to such technical and hard to understand data

01:29.990 --> 01:33.950
model, we go as developers creating multiple views.

01:33.990 --> 01:39.510
Since we are the expert of the data model, and these new views are going to be an abstraction of the

01:39.510 --> 01:46.430
complexity that I have in my database, and we have to make sure that those views are providing objects

01:46.430 --> 01:48.070
that are friendly.

01:48.070 --> 01:51.350
So they have like a full English name that makes sense.

01:51.350 --> 01:56.710
And as well the columns are friendly and we try to not offer a lot of views, so the user don't have

01:56.710 --> 01:57.910
to do all the joins.

01:57.910 --> 02:05.070
So we provide like few views that are friendly and has a lot of informations that the users needs for

02:05.070 --> 02:05.990
the analysis.

02:05.990 --> 02:11.270
So with that the users can have an access to something more friendly and easy to consume, and then

02:11.270 --> 02:16.670
they can write simple queries in order to do analysis on top of these friendly views.

02:16.710 --> 02:18.470
And this is what we can give a name like.

02:18.470 --> 02:24.190
We are providing a data product from my complex physical database.

02:24.270 --> 02:30.750
So here again how important are the views to provide an abstraction and easy to consume objects for

02:30.750 --> 02:31.510
the users?

02:31.510 --> 02:37.590
And with that I can hide all my complexity and the script of the view is going to be developed from

02:37.590 --> 02:44.830
the experts and only once, so that the users don't have to understand or to write these complex SQL

02:44.870 --> 02:45.510
joins.

02:45.510 --> 02:49.150
And with that, you can make your data projects way easier than before.

02:49.270 --> 02:54.750
So this is another important use case for the views, where we can use it in order to provide abstraction

02:54.750 --> 02:58.230
and as well, easy and friendly objects for the end users.

02:58.270 --> 02:58.430
Okay.

